<strong>NoSQL injection</strong>. If you have ever Googled =E2=80=9C<strong=
>what is NoSQL injection</strong>=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9C<strong>NoSQL inject=
ion</strong>,=E2=80=9D you already know it targets the flexible query model=
s behind today=E2=80=99s apps and APIs. That flexibility is powerful, but i=
t can be abused.</p><p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph">The risk is not theoret=
ical. Verizon=E2=80=99s 2024 DBIR counted 10,626 confirmed breaches, with w=
eb applications remaining a leading way in. Injection continues to play a m=
ajor part. Bright=E2=80=99s developer-first DAST and our Bright STAR platfo=
rm make finding, fixing, and verifying these issues part of your CI flow, s=
o vulnerable code never ships.</p><h2 id=3D"data" class=3D"wp-block-heading=
"><strong>What Is NoSQL Injection?</strong></h2><p class=3D"wp-block-paragr=
aph"><strong>NoSQL injection</strong> happens when untrusted input is inser=
ted into a NoSQL query, changing its logic. It is similar in spirit to clas=
sic SQL injection, but targets document, key-value, or search stores (for e=
xample MongoDB, Redis, or Elasticsearch). With NoSQL operators like $ne, $g=
t, or $regex, an attacker can bypass logins, read or modify data, or cause =
denial of service.</p><p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Why NoSQL=E2=
=80=99s flexibility is a risk:</strong> NoSQL engines accept rich, JSON-lik=
e filters and even JavaScript in some cases. That flexibility is great for =
developers, but if parameters are built directly from user input, operators=
 can be smuggled into queries. OWASP=E2=80=99s testing guide highlights dan=
gerous areas such as MongoDB=E2=80=99s $where and unserialized inputs.</p><=
h2 id=3D"leaked" class=3D"wp-block-heading"><strong>Exploitation Techniques=
: How Attackers Use NoSQL Injection</strong></h2><ol class=3D"wp-block-list=
"><li><strong>Query manipulation</strong><strong><br></strong>Attackers inj=
ect operators into filters to force logic to evaluate true (for example, {=
=E2=80=9Cuser=E2=80=9D:{=E2=80=9C$ne=E2=80=9D:null}}). They may chain boole=
an conditions to enumerate data.<br></li><li><strong>Authentication bypass<=
/strong><strong><br></strong>By abusing operators like $or, $regex, or $ne,=
 an attacker can trick the login check to accept any password. Labs and wri=
te-ups show practical payloads for admin takeover.<br></li><li><strong>Data=
 exfiltration</strong><strong><br></strong>Blind techniques use timing or b=
oolean responses to extract secrets record by record. Public bug-bounty rep=
orts document token leakage and privilege escalation through NoSQLi.<br></l=
i><li><strong>Regex injection / ReDoS</strong><strong><br></strong> Feeding=
 a catastrophic regex (for example, ^(a+)+$) into a $regex filter can lock =
the CPU, leading to service degradation or downtime.<br></li></ol><h3 class=
=3D"wp-block-heading"><strong>Summary table</strong></h3><figure class=3D"w=
p-block-table"><table class=3D"has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Att=
ack Type</strong></td><td><strong>Example Payload</strong></td><td><strong>=
Potential Impact</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Query Manipulation</td><td>{=E2=
=80=9Cusername=E2=80=9D:{=E2=80=9C$ne=E2=80=9D:null}}</td><td>Enumerate use=
rs, skip checks</td></tr><tr><td>Authentication Bypass</td><td>{=E2=80=9Cus=
er=E2=80=9D:=E2=80=9Dadmin=E2=80=9D,=E2=80=9Dpass=E2=80=9D:{=E2=80=9C$regex=
=E2=80=9D:=E2=80=9D.*=E2=80=9D}}</td><td>Log in without a password</td></tr=
><tr><td>Data Exfiltration</td><td>{=E2=80=9Cemail=E2=80=9D:{=E2=80=9C$rege=
x=E2=80=9D:=E2=80=9D^a.*=E2=80=9D}} + binary search loops</td><td>Extract d=
ata via responses</td></tr><tr><td>Regex Injection (ReDoS)</td><td>{=E2=80=
=9Cname=E2=80=9D:{=E2=80=9C$regex=E2=80=9D:=E2=80=9D^(a+)+$=E2=80=9D}}</td>=
<td>CPU spike, app unresponsive</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><p class=
=3D"wp-block-paragraph"><em>Tip:</em> Store these in a safe =E2=80=9Cpayloa=
d bank=E2=80=9D for testing, not production.</p><h2 id=3D"code" class=3D"wp=
-block-heading"><strong>Testing and Identifying NoSQL Injection Vulnerabili=
ties</strong></h2><h3 class=3D"wp-block-heading"><strong>Manual testing</st=
rong></h3><ul class=3D"wp-block-list"><li><strong>Payload injection:</stron=
g> Try JSON operators in parameters ($ne, $gt, $or, $regex), and watch for =
changes in query behavior.<br></li><li><strong>Fuzzing &amp; tampering:</st=
rong> Toggle parameter types (string, array, object), add unexpected keys, =
and observe error timing or status codes.<br></li><li><strong>Auth-path foc=
us:</strong> Exercise password reset, search, filtering, and admin listing =
endpoints.</li></ul><p class=3D"wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Where to look:<=
/strong> Follow OWASP WSTG for NoSQL testing specifics, including $where ha=
zards and unserialized inputs.<a href=3D"https://owasp.org/www-project-web-=
security-testing-guide/latest/4-Web_Application_Security_Testing/07-Input_V=
alidation_Testing/05.6-Testing_for_NoSQL_Injection?utm_source=3Dchatgpt.com=
" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener">&nbsp;</a></p><h3 class=3D"wp-block-he=
ading"><strong>Automated testing</strong></h3><ul class=3D"wp-block-list"><=
li><strong>DAST:</strong> Dynamic scanners send live payloads to your runni=
ng app and confirm exploitability. <a href=3D"https://brightsec.com/blog/sa=
st-vs-dast">Bright=E2=80=99s developer-first DAST</a> automatically validat=
es findings to keep false positives near zero.<br></li><li><strong>SAST:</s=
trong> Static analyzers flag string-built queries and unsafe deserializatio=
n early in the pipeline.<br></li><li><strong>IAST:</strong> Runtime instrum=
entation pinpoints vulnerable sinks with code context during testing.<a hre=
f=3D"https://brightsec.com/blog/iast-vs-dast-what-is-the-difference/"> See =
